  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 222 PM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: An upper-level low builds into the region today and \nremains over the area into Thursday. An associated surface low has \nformed over the Great Lakes and it will occlude as it moves into New \nYork and New England, bringing consistent showers. The widespread \nshowers have entered northern New York, and scattered showers and \nthunderstorms have developed across the higher terrain in Vermont. \nAs the afternoon and evening progress, the combination of \nstrengthening synoptic dynamics and increasing instability will \ncause these showers to become widespread. A lack of deep-layer is \nexpected to prevent any severe threat though. These storms will \nlikely have the highest rainfall rates of the entire event, but dry \nantecedent conditions and some south to north storm motion should \nkeep the flood threat relatively low through this afternoon. The \naxis of rain will become more sse-nnw oriented heading into this \nevening and night, and mostly stall in the same locations. However, \nas the evening goes on, this axis of rain looks to become a more \nbroken line, so there will be rounds of showers heading over \nthe areas but likely not constant rain. While the precipitation \nshould trend toward more stratiform as the night goes on, some \nconvective enhancement will still be likely, especially in \nsouthern Vermont where the highest totals are expected. This \nTuesday night into Wednesday morning would likely have the \nhighest concern for any flash flooding as the axis of rain looks\nto stay relatively stationary. Heading through tomorrow, the \ndynamics weaken and the axis of rain looks to become more broken\nand lighter. Periods of showers will continue tomorrow night \nthrough Thursday, but they will become much lighter and \ngradually less widespread as the surface low weakens and pushes \neast. The area of highest concern is the eastern slopes and \nspine of the southern Green Mountains where the best dynamics \nwill combine with upsloping. The east slopes of the Adirondacks \nwill likely be the second area of focus due to the upsloping. \nMost areas will see between 1-3 inches of rain, but isolated \nareas up to 5 inches are possible in parts of the southern \nGreens. Still, in the more favored areas, only isolated to \nscattered flash flooding looks to be possible. The Champlain \nValley and Northeast Kingdom will likely receive some locally \nlower totals due to downsloping. Showers will become much more \nisolated heading into Friday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Brief ridging across the region will continue to bring \ndrier conditions for the start of the weekend. Seasonably warm \ntemperatures are expected for the start of this weekend, with high \ntemperatures in 80s areawide. Unsettled weather conditions are \nexpected to return for the latter half of the weekend into the \nbeginning of next week as another shortwave trough moves through, \nalthough there is still some uncertainty regarding the timing of \nthis feature and how widespread showers will be. Temperatures will \nlargely be influenced by any cloud cover and precipitation, but \nabove normal temperatures are anticipated with highs climbing into \nthe mid 70s and 80s with overnight lows in the upper 50s and 60s.",
